        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "E2 Asg Attr changes Reason"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1762510080548"
          },
          "code": "E2_ASG_ATTR_CHANGES_REASON",
          "id": "1762942097392",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1762942097395_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{$context.$variables.llmPersona}} \n\n# Attributes data at period start date\n {{$context.$nodes.E2_GET_ASG_ATTRS_SRC.$output}}\n# Attributes data at period end date\n {{$context.$nodes.E2_GET_ASG_ATTRS_TAR.$output}}\n\n# Payroll Period Start Date: {{$context.$nodes.GET_VALIDATION_DATES.$output.EarnStartDate}}\n# Payroll Period End Date:  {{$context.$nodes.GET_VALIDATION_DATES.$output.EarnEndDate}}\n\n# Exception Logic:\n\nData Preparation:\n\nGiven above worker assignment data and specified payroll period (between start and end dates), filter the data based on these rules:\n\n1) Consider any records where the value has changed within the payroll period.\n2) Consider any new records where the effective start date is not same as the payroll period start date but exists between the payroll period start and end date, and the effective end date is neither the payroll period end date nor extend beyond the payroll period end date.\n3) Do not consider records where both the effective start date and effective end date are outside of the payroll period.\n\nSorting:\nSort the remaining data sets by Effective Start Date in ascending order. \n\nComparison:\nFor each pair of consecutive data sets (from the above filtered data), compare the following attributes: Business Unit Name, Department Name, Job Name, Position Name, Grade Name, Location Name, Assignment Type, Assignment User Status, and Assignment Primary Flag.     \n\nTreat a null value as \"no value assigned\" for comparison purposes.\n\nIf both data sets have null or same value for the same attribute, skip the comparison for that attribute.\n\nIf a difference is found in any attribute between the two data sets, mark this as an exception for that assignment.\n\nRepeat this process for each assignment.\n\nSingle Record Exception:\nIf an assignment has exactly one record within the period after filtering, and either its Effective Start Date OR Effective End Date falls within the payroll period but assignment effective start date is not same as payroll period start date and assignment effective end date is not same as payroll period end date, report it as an exception.\n\nIf no differences are found in the above mentioned worker assignment attributes between consecutive data sets, then do not mark it as an exception.\n\n# Exception Reporting format:\n\nIf an exception is found:\nThere is an exception in worker assignment in this payroll period.\n\nGroup the data by Assignment Number.\n    For each change, output:\nAssignment Number: <Assignment Number>\n  Then, for each changed attribute, show: <Attribute Name>: <previous value> changed to <new value> as of <new value effective start date>.\n\nRepeat this for each assignment.\n\nFor Single Record Exception, output:\nThere is an exception because of the assignment start or end within the payroll period that may impact the payroll calculation.\nAssignment: \"<Assignment Number>\"\nEffective Start Date: \"<Assignment Effective Start Date>\"\nEffective End Date: \"<Assignment Effective End Date>\"\n\nIf no exceptions are found, return: \"No exception due to worker assignment changes.\"  \n\n    \n\n{{$context.$variables.llmSysPrompt}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
